def midi
  title "MIDI in and out on 5-pin DIN for a 3.3 V MCU"
  about "MIDI IN: the sender's current loop through 220 Ω into a 6N138 optocoupler, a 1N4148 across its LED against a
      reversed loop, pin 2 of the socket open – the input stays isolated; the optocoupler's open-collector output is
      pulled up to the MCU's 3.3 V and goes to UART RX. MIDI OUT at 3.3 V: pin 4 through 33 Ω to 3.3 V, pin 5 through
      10 Ω to UART TX, pin 2 (shield) to ground. The resistor values are those of maker-coverage BL-11 for the MIDI
      Association's electrical specification (CA-033); the specification itself was not reachable while the block was
      built (see notes)."
  port rx "MIDI IN data – to the MCU's UART RX (31.25 kbit/s; idle high)"
  port tx "MIDI OUT data – from the MCU's UART TX (idle high)"
  port vio "the MCU's 3.3 V logic supply: pull-up of rx and the source of the MIDI OUT loop"
  port v5 "5 V for the optocoupler's detector (6N138: VCC 4.5–5.5 V)"
  port gnd "ground"
  part J1 connector SDS-50J lcsc C6216157 off_board "{as}: MIDI IN (DIN 5-pin 180°)"
    nc 1 2 3 shield
    notes "Pin 2 and the shell (E) open: the input has no galvanic connection to this board – the optocoupler isolates
      it."
  part R1 resistor 0805W8F2200T5E lcsc C17557 value 220 "{as}: MIDI IN series 220"
  part D1 diode 1N4148W lcsc C81598 "{as}: MIDI IN reverse diode 1N4148"
    notes "Across the optocoupler's LED, anode at pin 5: a reversed loop flows through it, the LED sees about 0.7 V
      (VR max 5 V)."
  part OK1 optocoupler 6N138-L lcsc C571211 "{as}: MIDI IN optocoupler 6N138"
    nc NC VB
    notes "Pin 7 (VB) open, as in every figure of the data sheet."
  part C1 capacitor CC0805KRX7R9BB104 lcsc C49678 value 100nF "{as}: 100 nF at the optocoupler's VCC"
    notes "Next to pins 8 and 5: the data sheet says it must be there."
  part R2 resistor 0805W8F1001T5E lcsc C17513 value 1k "{as}: RX pull-up 1 k"
  part J2 connector SDS-50J lcsc C6216157 off_board "{as}: MIDI OUT (DIN 5-pin 180°)"
    nc 1 3
    notes "Pin 2 and the shell (E) on ground: the shield of the cable."
  part R3 resistor 0805W8F330JT5E lcsc C17634 value 33 "{as}: MIDI OUT pin 4 to 3.3 V, 33"
  part R4 resistor 0805W8F100JT5E lcsc C17415 value 10 "{as}: MIDI OUT pin 5 to TX, 10"
  net in4 0..5.5 V J1.4 R1.1 isolated
    notes "The sender's current loop – isolated from this board's ground (net key isolated, on in4, led_a and in5):
      the range is the loop's own (a 5 V sender), the simulation does not hold it to this board's ground."
  net led_a 0..5.5 V R1.2 OK1.anode D1.cathode isolated
  net in5 0..5.5 V J1.5 OK1.cathode D1.anode isolated
  net rx @vio OK1.collector R2.2
  net tx @vio R4.2
  net out4 @vio J2.4 R3.2
  net out5 @vio J2.5 R4.1
  net vio R2.1 R3.1
  net v5 OK1.VCC C1.1
  net gnd OK1.emitter C1.2 J2.2 J2.shield
  covers REQ.missing/leave REQ.missing LVL.undefined/open_drain LIM.range HINT.esd
  notes "Source of the values: 220 Ω at MIDI IN, 1N4148 reverse diode, pin 2 open, and 33 Ω / 10 Ω at MIDI OUT for 3.3
      V (220 Ω / 220 Ω at 5 V) are taken from maker-coverage BL-11 and the task, which name the MIDI Association's
      MIDI 1.0 Electrical Specification Update (CA-033, 2014) as their source. midi.org could not be reached from the
      environment the block was built in (proxy 403): the specification was not read – check the values against it
      before a board is ordered."
  notes "MIDI OUT for 5 V: R3 and R4 both 220 Ω (0805W8F2200T5E, C17557) and vio on 5 V – the TX pin must then swing
      to 5 V."
  notes "MIDI OUT current, against a receiver built like this block's MIDI IN (220 Ω, 6N138 LED with VF 1.1–1.7 V at
      1.6 mA): about (3.3 V − 1.4 V − VOL) / (33 + 10 + 220) Ω ≈ 7 mA, which the TX pin sinks – inside the Pico 2's 12
      mA (gpio_i_max_a); on an RP2350 or ESP32 set the pin's drive strength to match. The receiver is off the board:
      edakit sim does not see this current."
  notes "A short of MIDI OUT pin 4 to ground (a damaged cable) puts 3.3 V across R3: 0.33 W – above the 1/8 W of an
      0805; take a larger resistor (1206 or bigger) where that must be survived."
  notes "Optocoupler 6N138-L: its characteristics hold for VCC 4.5–5.5 V (Lite-On DS70-2009-0001, p. 6) – so VCC is on
      v5, while the open-collector output is pulled up to vio (3.3 V). R2 1 kΩ: at IF 1.6 mA the CTR (≥ 300 %) sinks
      4.8 mA, more than the 3.3 mA of 1 kΩ at 3.3 V – the output reaches its VOL (≤ 0.4 V) from 1.6 mA of loop current
      on. A smaller pull-up shortens the rising edge (tPLH ≤ 35 µs, typ. 10 µs at 1.6 mA and 2.2 kΩ – p. 7; Broadcom's
      figure 7: about 1.6 µs at 12 mA and 270 Ω) but needs more LED current."
  notes "Speed at 31.25 kbit/s (32 µs per bit) is not guaranteed by the data sheet at this operating point: look at RX
      with a scope. If the rising edge is too slow: a smaller R2, or a resistor of 47 kΩ or more from pin 7 to pin 5
      (Broadcom 6N138 note 4: below 47 kΩ the gain drops markedly)."
  notes "On a board without 5 V the 6N138 is outside its specified supply; an optocoupler specified at 3.3 V is then
      the choice – not in this block."
  notes "The SMD version 6N138S-TA1-L (C22451885) has the same data sheet; its EasyEDA symbol calls the anode '2' and
      the cathode 'NC' – the block takes the DIP-8 6N138-L (C571211) whose symbol is right."
  notes "J1 and J2 are marked off_board: REQ.missing/leave sees R1 and R4 between the wires and the board; pin 4 of
      MIDI OUT goes to the 3.3 V rail through R3."
  notes "sim.json: rx under the MCU's inputs, tx under its outputs (idle high). The connected devices are off the
      board – the optocoupler's LED and the MIDI OUT loop carry no current in the simulation."
  set test {
     "connect": {
      "rx": "SIG2",
      "tx": "SIG",
      "vio": "3V3",
      "v5": "+5V"
     },
     "why": "the example's +5V is USB behind its SS34 and sinks to 4.44 V with all LEDs on at USB 4.75 V – a rail of the example, not of the block: the test leaves out the supply case usb-min – below the 4.5 V from which the 6N138's characteristics hold (Lite-On DS70-2009-0001, p. 6)",
     "remove_supplies": [
      "usb-min"
     ]
     }
